   "sdr_rx": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Not an SDR. The CC1101 is a fixed-function narrowband transceiver - you tune it to a frequency and modulation, you do not get IQ samples."
   },
   "sdr_tx": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Same - no arbitrary waveform generation."
   },
   "subghz_tx": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Region lock removed across the CC1101's rated 300-348 / 387-464 / 779-928 MHz bands. A settings file (subghz/assets/setting_user) can add custom frequencies and push beyond the rated range, which the project itself warns can damage the hardware. Legality is on you."
   },
   "subghz_rolling_code": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Be precise here. Community firmware lets you SAVE and re-send captured rolling-code signals, and adds manual creation/pairing for protocols like KeeLoq, FAAC SLH, BFT Mitto, Somfy Telis, Nice Flor-S, CAME Atomo and Security+ 2.0 - that is programming a NEW remote into a receiver you control, which needs physical access to the receiver's learn button. It does NOT let you clone someone's rolling-code remote from a captured press. The genuine exception is the RollBack class of attack (Csikor et al., arXiv:2210.11923): replaying two or more consecutively captured codes can roll a receiver's counter back and make old codes valid again. That is implemented openly in the 'rolling_flaws' app and is what the 2025 'DarkWeb firmware' stories were repackaging. Even where it works it only reaches the LOCK/UNLOCK remote-keyless-entry function on some brands - it does NOT touch the immobiliser, so it does not start a car."
   },
   "subghz_bruteforce": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Sub-GHz Bruteforcer app included. Only meaningful against short STATIC codes - 8-12 bit DIP-switch style remotes and some fixed-code gates. It will not brute-force a rolling code in any useful timeframe."
   },
   "mifare_classic_attacks": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Best-in-class for a device this cheap, but know the boundary. On device: dictionary attack against known keys, and the mfkey32 READER attack (emulate the card at a real reader, harvest nonces, recover Crypto-1 keys in a few minutes). Community firmware also adds card-only nested attack collection. What still generally needs a PC is the heavy maths: hardnested and full nested recovery, where the Flipper collects nonces into a .nested.log and a desktop tool or web service does the cracking. Static nested can be finished on device. Once keys are known it emulates the card and can write to 'magic' cards."
   },
   "nfc_type4_ndef": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "This is a genuine community-firmware-only capability. The forks add Type4Tag and Ntag4xx protocol modules that official firmware does not have: they READ NTAG 4xx and generic ISO14443-4 Type 4 tags, and there is a Type4Tag LISTENER, so the Flipper can present itself as an NDEF Type 4 tag (this is what the NFC Maker app uses to serve URLs, Wi-Fi credentials, contact cards). Note Ntag4xx itself is read-only - its listener entry is NULL - and DESFire emulation does not exist on any firmware."
   },
   "emv_read": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Community firmware has a real EMV poller, so it can read the openly-readable side of a contactless bank card: PAN, expiry, cardholder name where present, and sometimes a transaction log. That is NOT cloning - it cannot produce the dynamic cryptogram a terminal demands, so it cannot pay. Worth knowing: the EMV plugin was silently BROKEN for a long stretch - it shipped as a plugin that never loaded at all - and was only fixed in Unleashed PR #1073, merged 2026-08-20, then flowed downstream. If you used a build from before late August 2026, EMV appeared to be supported but did nothing."
   },
   "hf_1356_emulate": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Emulation exists for ISO14443-3A, ISO14443-4A, ISO15693-3, MIFARE Ultralight, MIFARE Classic, MIFARE Plus, SLIX, FeliCa and generic Type 4 Tag. Still NULL and therefore impossible: ISO14443-3B, ISO14443-4B, DESFire, ST25TB, NTAG4xx."
   },
   "magspoof": {
    "v": "addon",
    "note": "MagSpoof app is bundled, but it needs an external coil/H-bridge on the GPIO header to actually emit a magstripe field."
   },
   "usb_mass_storage": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Mass Storage app bundled - the Flipper presents an SD-card image as a USB drive."
   },
   "spi_flash_dump": {
    "v": "addon",
    "note": "SPI Mem Manager app is bundled; you still need test clips/wires to reach the chip."
   },
   "logic_analyzer": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Bundled community app. Low sample rate - fine for UART/I2C at modest speeds, not a bench analyser."
   },
   "ble_scan": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Community BLE scanner/tracker-detector apps exist in the app packs; not a core firmware feature."
   },
   "ble_spam_apple": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Apple Continuity. Note the difference from Momentum: Unleashed does NOT compile BLE Spam into the firmware - it ships in the separate all-the-plugins app pack you copy to the SD card. Real, but oversold. The STM32WB55 BLE stack advertises one payload at a time and cycles through them, so this is protocol-level nuisance advertising, not RF jamming and not a flood. Apple (iOS 17.2+), Google, Microsoft and Samsung all added cooldowns and sanity checks after 2023, so on current phones it is mostly a party trick. The app's own author describes it that way."
   },
   "ble_spam_android": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Google Fast Pair. Note the difference from Momentum: Unleashed does NOT compile BLE Spam into the firmware - it ships in the separate all-the-plugins app pack you copy to the SD card. Real, but oversold. The STM32WB55 BLE stack advertises one payload at a time and cycles through them, so this is protocol-level nuisance advertising, not RF jamming and not a flood. Apple (iOS 17.2+), Google, Microsoft and Samsung all added cooldowns and sanity checks after 2023, so on current phones it is mostly a party trick. The app's own author describes it that way."
   },
   "ble_spam_samsung": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Samsung EasySetup. Note the difference from Momentum: Unleashed does NOT compile BLE Spam into the firmware - it ships in the separate all-the-plugins app pack you copy to the SD card. Real, but oversold. The STM32WB55 BLE stack advertises one payload at a time and cycles through them, so this is protocol-level nuisance advertising, not RF jamming and not a flood. Apple (iOS 17.2+), Google, Microsoft and Samsung all added cooldowns and sanity checks after 2023, so on current phones it is mostly a party trick. The app's own author describes it that way."
   },
   "ble_spam_swift_pair": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Microsoft Swift Pair. Note the difference from Momentum: Unleashed does NOT compile BLE Spam into the firmware - it ships in the separate all-the-plugins app pack you copy to the SD card. Real, but oversold. The STM32WB55 BLE stack advertises one payload at a time and cycles through them, so this is protocol-level nuisance advertising, not RF jamming and not a flood. Apple (iOS 17.2+), Google, Microsoft and Samsung all added cooldowns and sanity checks after 2023, so on current phones it is mostly a party trick. The app's own author describes it that way."
   },
   "findmy_track": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Read this carefully because the name misleads everyone: FindMy Flipper does NOT find or track other people's AirTags. It turns the Flipper itself INTO a trackable tag, broadcasting a BLE beacon that Apple's Find My network (or Samsung SmartThings / Tile) picks up, so YOU can locate your own Flipper. Setup is either cloning an existing tag's MAC and payload (which requires killing the original tag's battery so it stops rotating keys) or generating OpenHaystack key pairs with Docker/Python on a PC and importing a .keys file. It is an app, not a radio feature - the underlying hardware capability is just 'BLE can advertise'. Unleashed compiles this one in (applications/system/find_my_flipper), as it does the mfkey cracker."
   },
   "ble_tracker_detect": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Separate community apps scan for tracker advertisements and match on service UUID / manufacturer data. Not the same thing as FindMy Flipper, and not a firmware feature."
   }
